{
  "abstract": "Let b(n, l) be the number of parking functions of length n whose maximum displacement is exactly l, where the displacement of a car is the distance between the spot it takes and its preferred spot. The formalization proves in Lean 4 with Mathlib that for every n >= 1 the sequence b(n, 0), b(n, 1), ..., b(n, n-1) is unimodal, which is Conjecture 6.5 of Celano, Elder, Hadaway, Harris, Martin, Priestley and Udell (arXiv:2507.07243). The numbers b(n, l) are defined directly from the parking process. The proof starts from a recursion for the number of parking functions whose maximum displacement is at most a given bound, proved in the library from the parking process, and follows the sign of the difference b(n, k) - b(n, k-1) along columns k as n grows. Rows with n <= 16 and several finite ranges are settled by tables that Lean's kernel evaluates in exact integer arithmetic; analytic estimates, including Robbins' bounds for the factorial, settle the remaining ranges. The proof depends only on the axioms propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. It was found, written and formalized by AI sessions of an autonomous research system; no person has refereed it.",
